## Environment Configuration

Use a dedicated workspace per environment so state is fully isolated (AC #7):

```bash
terraform workspace new dev
terraform workspace select dev
terraform init
terraform plan -var-file=envs/dev.tfvars -out=tfplan
terraform apply tfplan
```

Workspaces give each environment its own state file, so changing prod cannot touch dev.

**Best practice:** pass secrets out-of-band rather than committing them:

```bash
export TF_VAR_db_password="$(sops -d envs/secrets.enc.yaml | yq '.db_password')"
terraform plan -var-file=envs/prod.tfvars
```

### `envs/dev.tfvars`

```hcl
region      = "us-east-1"
environment = "dev"
project     = "myapp"

bucket_name = "myapp-dev-assets-482910577342"  # unique suffix

db_instance_class     = "db.t3.micro"
db_multi_az           = false
db_storage_encrypted  = true

node_group_size = {
  desired_size   = 2
  min_size       = 2
  max_size       = 3
  instance_types = ["t3.medium"]
}

replication_enabled = false

# Dev uses programmatic IAM user access
oidc_provider_arn = ""
```

### `envs/prod.tfvars`

```hcl
region      = "us-east-1"
environment = "prod"
project     = "myapp"

bucket_name = "myapp-prod-assets-482910577342"

db_instance_class     = "db.t3.medium"
db_multi_az           = true
db_storage_encrypted  = true

node_group_size = {
  desired_size   = 4
  min_size       = 4
  max_size       = 6
  instance_types = ["t3.large"]
}

replication_enabled = true
replica_region      = "us-west-2"

# Prod uses OIDC trust (IRSA) — least privilege, no static keys
oidc_provider_arn = "arn:aws:iam::482910577342:oidc-provider/oidc.eks.us-east-1.amazonaws.com"
oidc_issuer_url   = "https://oidc.eks.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/id/EXAMPLED539D4633E53DE1B71EXAMPLE"
```

*Alternative without workspaces:* swap `backend.tf` and `-state=` flags per env:

```bash
terraform init -backend-config="key=myapp/dev/terraform.tfstate" \
  terraform plan -var-file=envs/dev.tfvars
```

Each command uses an independent state backend per environment regardless of approach chosen.

