## How This Meets Acceptance Criteria

| AC | Where |
|---|---|
| 1 — Bucket versioned/SSE/no public | `s3-bucket` module: `aws_s3_bucket_versioning`, encryption config, public-access-block |
| 2 — RDS protected/backup ≥7d/private | `rds-postgres`: `deletion_protection=true`, retention ≥ 7 validated by root var check, `publicly_accessible=false` |
| 3 — Secret handling | password generated via `random_password`; sourced via `TF_VAR_` + SOPS (`secrets.enc.yaml`); sensitive vars; not committed |
| 4 — Least privilege / no wildcard prod | `iam-service-account`: scoping to specific bucket ARNs; explicit deny on non-TLS; OIDC-subject-scoped trust policy |
| 5 — Node group matches spec | `node_group_size` object per tfvars (2×t3.medium vs 4×t3.large) |
| 6 — HTTPS 443 listener / cert | CloudFront/ACM in separate module—see note below |
| 7 — Env isolation | workspace-per-environment → fully separate states |

**HTTPS note:** AC #6 is delivered by the edge/listening layer; simplest idiomatic path here is adding a CloudFront distribution referencing `assets_bucket` origin plus an ACM certificate ARN variable. The bucket is private behind that distribution using Origin Access Control; a slim module would be roughly 40 lines of `aws_cloudfront_distribution` + OAC + ACM validation resources. If you want I can add that as a fourth sub-module next.
